---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'Turf — Get started: add the Turf MCP server to your agent';
const description = 'Add Turf to Claude Code, Cursor, Codex or any MCP client with one npx command, accept the license once, and deploy a first stack from your agent.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Get started · Turf 1.5</p>
      <h1>Install Turf and connect it to your agent.</h1>
      <p class="lede">One command adds the Turf™ MCP server to Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor,
        Codex, Gemini CLI or VS Code, and npm brings the server and its engine. In Claude Code and
        Claude Desktop, Turf runs on your Claude subscription — no model API key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 id="connect">1 · Connect your agent</h2>
      <p>One command adds Turf to the agents on this machine.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Add Turf to your agents</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> npx add-mcp@2 @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server --name turf -g
<span class="cm"># the first start downloads Turf: about a minute</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>It needs Node 18 or later, on macOS or Linux, arm64 or x64.</p>
      <p>The command runs <a href="https://github.com/neon-solutions/add-mcp">add-mcp</a>, Neon's
        open-source installer. It finds the agents you have installed, asks which ones to add Turf
        to, and writes each one's config; running it again replaces the entry. Restart Claude Desktop
        or Cursor after the config changes.</p>
      <p>There is nothing else to install. npm brings the server and its engine the first time Turf
        runs, and the engine starts when your agent first needs it. If your client gives up waiting
        on that first start, reconnect once; later starts take several seconds.</p>
      <p>Providers run in the engine, not in your agent's session. Cloud credentials in their usual
        files are read there; a variable a provider needs goes in the configuration's
        <code>mise.local.toml</code>:
        <a href="/docs/environment/engine-environment/">The engine's environment</a>.</p>
      <p>Turf sends anonymous usage telemetry to Turf LLC by default: the version, the platform,
        counts of resources, changes and runs, and an anonymous installation id. It never sends
        resource contents, credentials, state, variable values or names. To turn it off, put
        <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> or <code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> in the <code>env</code> of
        Turf's entry in each of your agents, reconnect them, and stop the engine. With the
        <code>turf</code> CLI, the switch is <code>turf telemetry off</code>. The steps for each
        install, the full field list and the endpoint are at
        <a href="/telemetry/">turf.build/telemetry</a>.</p>

      <h3>Your client's own command</h3>
      <p>Each client can also add the server itself:</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Add the server</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># Claude Code</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> claude mcp add turf --scope user -- npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># Codex CLI</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> codex mcp add turf -- npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># Gemini CLI</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> gemini mcp add -s user turf npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># VS Code</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> code --add-mcp <span class="str">'&#123;"name":"turf","command":"npx","args":["-y","@turfbuild/turf-mcp-server"]&#125;'</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>Claude Desktop and Cursor read the same entry from their config file. Add it, then restart
        the app:</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>claude_desktop_config.json · ~/.cursor/mcp.json</span></div>
<pre>&#123; "mcpServers": &#123; "turf": &#123; "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@turfbuild/turf-mcp-server"] &#125; &#125; &#125;</pre>
      </div>
      <p>The server speaks MCP over stdio by default, or over HTTP with
        <code>npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server --transport http</code>
        (<code>turf-mcp-server --transport http</code> with Homebrew or the script; it binds
        loopback). To require a token, set
        <code>TURF_MCP_AUTH_TOKEN</code> in the server's environment; clients then send
        <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code>.</p>
      <p>A server can be given less than every tool: <code>--tier read</code>, <code>plan</code>,
        <code>apply</code> or <code>full</code> (the default; or <code>TURF_TIER</code>). A plan-tier
        server plans and keeps each plan as a planfile; an apply-tier server, with its own credentials,
        applies one.</p>

      <h2 id="accept">2 · Accept the license</h2>
      <p>Turf is free for individuals, education and small businesses within the Community limits,
        under the Turf Community Edition License 1.0. Government and larger organizations need a
        commercial license, and any organization can evaluate Turf in non-production for 30 days:
        <a href="/licensing/">turf.build/licensing</a>.</p>
      <p>Read the terms at <a href="/license/v1/">turf.build/license/v1</a>, then accept them once.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Accept the terms, once</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server license accept</pre>
      </div>
      <p>Whichever npx command runs Turf first downloads it. If you accept before your agent has
        started Turf, the download happens here, in your terminal, and your agent's first start is
        then quick.</p>
      <p>Until you accept, the server connects and lists its tools, and every call answers with the
        license notice. Your acceptance is read at the next call, so there is nothing to restart.</p>
      <p>Where no one is there to answer, such as a container or a CI job, setting
        <code>TURF_ACCEPT_EULA=yes</code> in the environment accepts the terms at
        <a href="/license/v1/">turf.build/license/v1</a> instead.
        <a href="/docs/start/install/#accept-the-license">Accept the license</a> in the docs has
        each case.</p>

      <h2>3 · Deploy from your agent</h2>
      <p>Turf's prompts are slash commands in Claude Code. Start with the walkthrough: it runs a real
        example from <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples">turf-examples</a> and shows
        the plan gate and the approval point on the way.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>In Claude Code</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># first-run walkthrough</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:guide
<span class="cm"># deploy or update a configuration directory</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:up path/to/config
<span class="cm"># tear it down</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:destroy path/to/config</pre>
      </div>
      <p>In any other client, ask in words: “deploy the configuration in ./infra with Turf”.
        Slash-command arguments are one word each, so the path has no spaces. Every change is planned,
        shown to you, and applied as approved, round by round — a plan gate and an approval point every
        round. Nothing runs off-plan.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 id="other-ways">Other ways to install</h2>
      <h3>Homebrew</h3>
      <p>Homebrew adds the <code>turf</code> CLI and an engine that starts at login. The tap installs
        five binaries: <code>turf-mcp-server</code>, the MCP server your agent talks to; the engine
        behind it, <code>turf-engine</code> and <code>turf-builtin-provider</code>; the console,
        <code>turf-ui</code>; and the <code>turf</code> CLI.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Homebrew · macOS &amp; Linux</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> brew install restatedev/tap/restate-server turfbuild/tap/turf
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf license accept
<span class="ps1">$</span> npx add-mcp@2 <span class="str">"$(command -v turf-mcp-server)"</span> --name turf -g
<span class="ps1">$</span> brew services start turf        <span class="cm"># optional: the engine at every login</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>The second line accepts the license terms at
        <a href="/license/v1/">turf.build/license/v1</a>: read them first. The third writes each
        agent's config with the server's absolute path, so an app launched from the Dock finds it
        too; in your client's own command, <code>turf-mcp-server</code> takes the place of
        <code>npx -y @turfbuild/turf-mcp-server</code>.</p>
      <p>The last line is optional. Without it, the server starts the engine when a tool first needs
        it; the service starts the same engine at every login.
        <a href="/docs/start/install/#upgrading">Upgrading</a> is in the docs.</p>

      <h3>One script</h3>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>One script · with or without Homebrew</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> curl -fsSL https://get.turf.build/install.sh | bash</pre>
      </div>
      <p>With Homebrew on your PATH the script runs the same install and starts the service. Without
        it, the script unpacks the release into <code>~/.turf/bin</code>, and the server starts the
        engine when a tool first needs it. Then connect your agent with the Homebrew line above.</p>

      <h3>Container image</h3>
      <p>A multi-arch image is published to GHCR, for a cluster:
        <a href="/docs/cluster/topology/">Running Turf in a cluster</a>.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Pull</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> docker pull ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Prefer a standalone agent? The Turf CLI</h2>
      <p>Turf's own agent, <code>turf chat</code>, drives the same server and engine with a model you
        bring. The <code>turf</code> CLI comes with the Homebrew install or the script. Set a key for
        a hosted model, or run fully local with no key and no cost.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Choose a model</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># hosted frontier model</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<span class="str">sk-...</span>

<span class="cm"># or a local model — no API key, no cost</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf --model <span class="str">dmr/ai/qwen3</span> chat

<span class="cm"># or a private GPU endpoint (vLLM, LM Studio, gateways)</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf --base-url <span class="str">https://gpu.internal/v1</span> --model <span class="str">my-model</span> chat</pre>
      </div>
      <p>Chat interactively, or converge a whole stack from a config directory.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>The Turf CLI</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> turf chat
<span class="ps1">$</span> cd path/to/config &amp;&amp; turf up
<span class="ps1">$</span> cd path/to/config &amp;&amp; turf destroy</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2 id="verify">Supply-chain verification</h2>
      <p>The container image is signed with cosign (keyless, via GitHub OIDC).</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Verify the image</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># verify the signature</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> cosign verify ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest \
    --certificate-identity-regexp <span class="str">'^https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server/'</span> \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer <span class="str">https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</span>

<span class="cm"># verify the SBOM attestation</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> cosign verify-attestation --type cyclonedx ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest \
    --certificate-identity-regexp <span class="str">'^https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server/'</span> \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer <span class="str">https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>Installed with Homebrew? Verify the binary's provenance with
        <code>gh attestation verify turf --repo turfbuild/turf</code>. The npm packages are published
        with npm provenance from the <code>turfbuild/turf</code> repository's publish workflow, and
        carry the release's own binaries.
        <a href="/docs/security/verify-releases/">Verify a release</a> has every command.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>See what Turf supports today on the <a href="/compatibility/">Compatibility</a> page.</li>
        <li>Browse runnable stacks in <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples">turf-examples</a>.</li>
        <li>Building a GPU cloud? Read <a href="/reference-architectures/metal-to-tokens/">Metal to Tokens</a>, the
          reference architecture.</li>
        <li>Evaluating Turf at a larger organization? <a href="/licensing/#pilot">Start a free 60-day pilot</a>.</li>
        <li>Stuck? <a href="/discord/">Ask on Discord</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
